Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Gantry Weeps Creosote
- Story & Context: In the dead of December at the Longyearbyen Siding, night-clerk Einar accidentally knocked a steaming mug of black chicory coffee onto the automated ventilation panel of Vault Tier 4, sparking an intermittent alarm chime that looped endlessly through the cold concrete chambers. To quell his panic and soothe the millions of frozen wheat seeds trembling within their foil sleeves, Einar wired a children's celesta to a battery-powered 808, singing frantic pitch-bent hooks into the depot dispatch mic until the dawn signal-lamps flared amber. "The gantry weeps its creosote, don't leave me frozen by the tracks," bled across regional transport radios, sparking the first recorded bootleg of the scene.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A rusted iron railway gantry towering over snow-covered switch tracks in blue pre-dawn gloom, dripping thick black creosote into virgin white permafrost, framed by an open vault bulkhead glowing with red safety lanterns, captured on grainy cinematic 16mm film.

Track 2: Clerk Vane’s 03:14 Clearance
- Story & Context: Pacing the drafty dispatch cabin at the junction depot, Marja Vane clutched a stolen manifest while the bitter smell of burning engine oil and frozen grease burned her throat. She had falsified transport documents to load forty cases of extinct boreal seed pods onto the outbound night-freight, desperate to trade the archive's heritage for two train tickets to the southern coast before her contract expired. Recording her voice directly into a sampler using an acoustic telephone handset, she laid down the heartbroken refrain, "I signed your release at the platform gate, but the frost took the timetable first." The resulting confession became an underground anthem among transit workers who recognized the dread of missing the last departure.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: An interior shot of an unheated mid-century wooden railway stationmaster booth at night, frost creeping up the windowpanes, a solitary glowing green CRT monitor displaying a train timetable, beside an open velvet-lined wooden box holding a miniature celesta keyboard, cold cyan and amber tones.

Track 3: Has The Last Freight Cleared?
- Story & Context: Long after the Arctic depot rails were officially abandoned to the permafrost, rail-scavenger Astrid reported hearing hollow sub-bass rumbles shaking the rusted switch points every Sunday at twilight. Prying open the sealed concrete blast door to the old underground repository, she discovered the mechanical refrigeration units still faintly chattering, accompanied by an eerie, automated celesta loop broadcast from a battery that refused to die. The ghostly vocal chop drifted through the rusted exhaust chimney into the tundra wind: "Nobody waits where the sleepers split, nobody asks if the freight cleared out." Today, wandering train-hoppers leave stamped iron baggage tags on the frozen ties to ensure safe passage past the dead archive.
